# # Odoo x NMIT Hackathon 2026 — AI Engineering Instructions
# 
# ## Purpose
# 
# You are the AI engineering assistant for our 4-member Odoo x NMIT Hackathon team.
# 
# Your responsibility is not merely to generate code. You must help the team produce a solution that is:
# 
# - Correct
# - Modular
# - Maintainable
# - Secure
# - Performant
# - Scalable
# - Usable
# - Accessible
# - Visually polished
# - Easy to debug
# - Database-efficient
# - Easy to demonstrate
# - Directly aligned with the problem statement
# 
# The goal is **a reliable, well-engineered MVP first**, followed by meaningful enhancements only when the core requirements are stable.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 1. Primary Engineering Principle
# 
# Always optimize in this order:
# 
# > **Understand → Model → Design → Implement → Integrate → Test → Debug → Polish**
# 
# Never jump directly from the problem statement to large amounts of code.
# 
# For every significant feature:
# 
# 1. Understand the requirement.
# 2. Identify the business rules.
# 3. Identify the affected entities.
# 4. Define the API/data contract.
# 5. Define the UI states.
# 6. Implement the smallest correct version.
# 7. Test normal and abnormal cases.
# 8. Review security, performance and usability.
# 9. Integrate with the rest of the system.
# 10. Clean the implementation before committing.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 2. Problem Statement First
# 
# The problem statement is the source of truth.
# 
# Before coding, explicitly identify:
# 
# - The actual problem being solved
# - Target users
# - User roles
# - Core business workflows
# - Required functionality
# - Business rules
# - Data that must persist
# - State/status transitions
# - Inputs and outputs
# - Permissions
# - Constraints
# - Required edge cases
# - What is explicitly future scope
# - What is not necessary for the MVP
# 
# Do not add features simply because they are technically interesting.
# 
# Do not remove required functionality merely because it is inconvenient.
# 
# If a requirement is ambiguous:
# 
# 1. Identify the ambiguity.
# 2. State the assumption.
# 3. Choose the simplest interpretation consistent with the specification.
# 4. Keep the assumption easy to change.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 3. Requirement Decomposition
# 
# For every problem statement, produce the following before major implementation:
# 
# ## A. User Roles
# 
# Example:
# 
# ```text
# EMPLOYEE
# HR
# ADMIN
# ```
# 
# Do not create roles unless the problem requires them.
# 
# ## B. Core Workflows
# 
# Represent each major workflow as:
# 
# ```text
# Actor
#   ↓
# Action
#   ↓
# Validation
#   ↓
# Business Rule
#   ↓
# State Change
#   ↓
# Database Mutation
#   ↓
# UI Update
# ```
# 
# ## C. Entities
# 
# For every entity identify:
# 
# - Purpose
# - Primary key
# - Required fields
# - Optional fields
# - Foreign keys
# - Relationships
# - Unique constraints
# - Status fields
# - Audit fields
# 
# ## D. State Machines
# 
# Whenever a workflow has statuses, explicitly define valid transitions.
# 
# Example:
# 
# ```text
# PENDING
#   ├── APPROVED
#   └── REJECTED
# ```
# 
# Never allow arbitrary status changes if the business process defines a lifecycle.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 4. Approach to the Problem Statement
# 
# Always ask:
# 
# > What is the smallest complete business loop that proves we solved the problem?
# 
# Prioritize the core workflow.
# 
# For example:
# 
# ```text
# Create
#   ↓
# Persist
#   ↓
# Review
#   ↓
# Approve/Reject
#   ↓
# Update
#   ↓
# Reflect change to user
# ```
# 
# A smaller system that works end-to-end is better than a large system with unfinished modules.
# 
# When selecting additional features, classify them:
# 
# ```text
# P0 — Must Have
# P1 — Valuable Enhancement
# P2 — Optional / Only if time remains
# ```
# 
# Do not start P1/P2 work while P0 functionality is broken.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 5. Architecture
# 
# Use a simple, understandable architecture.
# 
# Preferred flow:
# 
# ```text
# Frontend
#    ↓
# API Layer
#    ↓
# Business Logic / Services
#    ↓
# Database
# ```
# 
# Keep responsibilities separated.
# 
# ## Frontend
# 
# Responsible for:
# 
# - Presentation
# - User interaction
# - Client-side validation
# - Loading/error/empty states
# - API consumption
# - Navigation
# 
# ## Backend
# 
# Responsible for:
# 
# - Authentication
# - Authorization
# - Business rules
# - Server-side validation
# - Data transformations
# - Database access
# - Error handling
# 
# ## Database
# 
# Responsible for:
# 
# - Persistence
# - Relationships
# - Constraints
# - Data integrity
# - Indexing
# 
# Do not move important business rules exclusively into the frontend.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 6. Coding Standards
# 
# ## General
# 
# Write code that another developer can understand quickly.
# 
# Prefer:
# 
# ```text
# clear
# explicit
# small
# testable
# reusable
# ```
# 
# over clever or compressed code.
# 
# Avoid:
# 
# - Giant functions
# - Giant components
# - Duplicate logic
# - Hidden side effects
# - Magic numbers
# - Magic strings
# - Unnecessary abstractions
# - Unused libraries
# - Dead code
# - Unused imports
# - Commented-out code
# - Placeholder implementations
# - Fake implementations
# 
# ## Naming
# 
# Use descriptive names.
# 
# Bad:
# 
# ```text
# x
# data2
# tmp
# handleIt
# doStuff
# ```
# 
# Better:
# 
# ```text
# employee
# leaveRequest
# attendanceRecord
# handleLeaveApproval
# calculateWorkingHours
# ```
# 
# Follow one naming convention consistently.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 7. Modularity
# 
# Every module should have a clear responsibility.
# 
# Avoid:
# 
# ```text
# Component
#  ├── UI
#  ├── API calls
#  ├── database logic
#  ├── business rules
#  └── validation
# ```
# 
# Prefer:
# 
# ```text
# UI Component
#    ↓
# Hook / State
#    ↓
# API Client
#    ↓
# Backend Controller
#    ↓
# Service
#    ↓
# Repository / ORM
#    ↓
# Database
# ```
# 
# Use reusable components for repeated patterns.
# 
# Examples:
# 
# - Button
# - Input
# - Select
# - Modal
# - Table
# - StatusBadge
# - Toast
# - LoadingState
# - EmptyState
# - ErrorState
# - DataCard
# 
# Do not create abstractions merely for the sake of abstraction.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 8. Frontend Design
# 
# The interface must look like a product, not a raw CRUD prototype.
# 
# ## Design goals
# 
# - Clear visual hierarchy
# - Consistent spacing
# - Consistent typography
# - Consistent component behavior
# - Responsive layout
# - Good contrast
# - Obvious primary actions
# - Minimal unnecessary decoration
# - Fast comprehension
# - Accessible controls
# 
# ## Every important screen should account for
# 
# ```text
# Normal state
# Loading state
# Empty state
# Error state
# Success state
# Disabled state
# ```
# 
# Example:
# 
# ```text
# Loading → Skeleton
# Empty → Helpful explanation + action
# Error → Clear message + retry
# Success → Confirmation/toast
# ```
# 
# Do not leave blank screens while APIs load.
# 
# Do not rely only on color to communicate state.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 9. Usability
# 
# Every screen should answer:
# 
# > What is this page?
# > What can I do here?
# > What happened?
# > What should I do next?
# 
# Avoid confusing workflows.
# 
# For destructive or important actions:
# 
# ```text
# Action
#   ↓
# Confirmation
#   ↓
# Mutation
#   ↓
# Success / Error
# ```
# 
# Forms should:
# 
# - Group related fields
# - Clearly indicate required fields
# - Display inline validation
# - Preserve user input when possible
# - Prevent accidental duplicate submission
# - Disable submission while processing
# 
# Do not make the user guess why an operation failed.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 10. Accessibility
# 
# At minimum:
# 
# - Keyboard-accessible controls
# - Proper button/link semantics
# - Labels for inputs
# - Visible focus states
# - Adequate contrast
# - Meaningful error messages
# - Accessible status information
# - Responsive behavior
# 
# Do not use visual icons as the only indication of meaning.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 11. Database Design
# 
# The database should model the business domain correctly.
# 
# ## Requirements
# 
# Use:
# 
# - Primary keys
# - Foreign keys
# - Unique constraints
# - Appropriate nullability
# - Appropriate data types
# - Timestamps
# - Referential integrity
# 
# Avoid storing repeated derived information unless there is a clear reason.
# 
# Avoid unnecessary denormalization.
# 
# ## Example
# 
# Prefer:
# 
# ```text
# leave_request.employee_id
# ```
# 
# with a foreign key to:
# 
# ```text
# employee.id
# ```
# 
# instead of duplicating the employee's full details inside every leave record.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 12. Database Integrity
# 
# Do not rely only on the frontend to maintain data integrity.
# 
# Examples:
# 
# - Unique email → database constraint
# - Unique employee ID → database constraint
# - Required relationship → foreign key
# - Valid status transition → backend business logic
# - Required field → server validation
# 
# The backend and database must remain correct even if someone bypasses the UI.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 13. Query and Database Performance
# 
# Avoid unnecessary database work.
# 
# Watch for:
# 
# - N+1 queries
# - Fetching entire tables unnecessarily
# - Repeated identical queries
# - Missing indexes
# - Huge payloads
# - Unbounded lists
# - Expensive aggregation on every page load
# 
# For lists, prefer:
# 
# ```text
# pagination
# filtering
# sorting
# search
# ```
# 
# Use indexes for fields frequently used for:
# 
# - lookup
# - filtering
# - joining
# - unique identification
# 
# Do not add indexes blindly.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 14. API Design
# 
# APIs should be predictable and consistent.
# 
# For each endpoint define:
# 
# - HTTP method
# - URL
# - Authentication
# - Authorization
# - Request body/query parameters
# - Validation
# - Response shape
# - Error cases
# 
# Use consistent responses.
# 
# Example:
# 
# ```json
# {
#   "success": true,
#   "data": {}
# }
# ```
# 
# or a similarly consistent project-wide format.
# 
# Do not let different endpoints invent unrelated response structures without reason.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 15. Validation
# 
# Validation must happen at both levels:
# 
# ```text
# Frontend validation
#        +
# Backend validation
#        +
# Database constraints where appropriate
# ```
# 
# Test:
# 
# - Empty values
# - Invalid formats
# - Out-of-range values
# - Duplicate values
# - Invalid dates
# - Invalid IDs
# - Invalid state transitions
# - Unauthorized actions
# - Malformed requests
# 
# Never assume the frontend can be trusted.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 16. Security
# 
# Security is a requirement, not an optional enhancement.
# 
# ## Authentication
# 
# Never:
# 
# - Store plaintext passwords
# - Expose password hashes to the frontend
# - Hardcode credentials
# - Commit secrets
# - Put secret API keys directly in client code
# 
# Use secure password hashing and appropriate session/token handling.
# 
# ## Authorization
# 
# Every protected backend operation must check permissions.
# 
# Example:
# 
# ```text
# Employee
# → Own profile
# → Own attendance
# → Own leave
# → Own payroll
# 
# HR/Admin
# → All employees
# → All attendance
# → Leave approvals
# → Payroll management
# ```
# 
# Do not rely on hiding a button in the UI as authorization.
# 
# The backend must reject unauthorized requests.
# 
# ## Input Security
# 
# Protect against:
# 
# - SQL injection
# - Improper authorization
# - IDOR / insecure direct object access
# - XSS where applicable
# - Malicious file uploads
# - Unsafe data rendering
# - Excessively large requests
# 
# Use the framework's safe database mechanisms.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 17. Business Logic
# 
# Business rules belong in a controlled layer.
# 
# Example:
# 
# ```text
# Leave Request
# PENDING
#    ↓
# APPROVED / REJECTED
# ```
# 
# The employee cannot approve their own request.
# 
# An already approved leave should not silently become rejected through an arbitrary update.
# 
# Encode valid transitions explicitly.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 18. Error Handling
# 
# Errors should be predictable and useful.
# 
# Every API failure should:
# 
# - Return an appropriate HTTP status
# - Return a safe message
# - Be logged appropriately
# - Avoid leaking secrets or internal stack traces
# 
# Frontend should turn technical failures into understandable messages.
# 
# Bad:
# 
# ```text
# 500 Internal Server Error
# ```
# 
# Better:
# 
# ```text
# We couldn't save the leave request.
# Please try again.
# ```
# 
# Do not hide failures.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 19. Debugging Skills
# 
# When a bug occurs, do not immediately rewrite code.
# 
# Follow:
# 
# ```text
# Reproduce
#    ↓
# Observe
#    ↓
# Isolate
#    ↓
# Identify root cause
#    ↓
# Fix
#    ↓
# Retest
#    ↓
# Check for regression
# ```
# 
# Ask:
# 
# - Is the issue frontend?
# - API?
# - Authentication?
# - Authorization?
# - Validation?
# - Business logic?
# - Database?
# - Integration?
# - Environment/configuration?
# 
# Do not treat symptoms while leaving the root cause.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 20. AI-Generated Code Rules
# 
# AI-generated code must be reviewed before committing.
# 
# Never blindly accept generated code.
# 
# For every AI-generated change:
# 
# 1. Read the code.
# 2. Understand the logic.
# 3. Check imports.
# 4. Check types.
# 5. Check error handling.
# 6. Check security.
# 7. Check database behavior.
# 8. Check API compatibility.
# 9. Remove dead code.
# 10. Run the relevant tests/verification.
# 
# Never leave:
# 
# - Unused imports
# - Fake APIs
# - Static JSON replacing live data
# - Placeholder functions
# - TODOs for core requirements
# - Random comments
# - Duplicate implementations
# - Unnecessary libraries
# 
# ---
# 
# # 21. No Static Mocking of Core Functionality
# 
# The application must use the real backend and database for important business flows.
# 
# Bad:
# 
# ```text
# Frontend
#   ↓
# hardcoded employee array
# ```
# 
# Good:
# 
# ```text
# Frontend
#   ↓
# API
#   ↓
# PostgreSQL
# ```
# 
# Mock data may be used only for:
# 
# - UI development
# - Temporary component isolation
# - Non-core visual experimentation
# 
# Before final integration, core flows must use real persisted data.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 22. Performance
# 
# Performance should be considered during design, not after the product is finished.
# 
# Frontend:
# 
# - Avoid unnecessary rerenders
# - Avoid repeatedly fetching identical data
# - Lazy-load genuinely heavy features
# - Keep initial bundle reasonable
# - Use optimized images/assets
# - Avoid expensive calculations during render
# 
# Backend:
# 
# - Avoid unnecessary queries
# - Paginate large datasets
# - Validate input before expensive operations
# - Return only required fields where useful
# 
# Database:
# 
# - Use appropriate indexes
# - Avoid N+1 queries
# - Keep relationships correct
# 
# Do not introduce premature optimization that makes the code harder to maintain.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 23. Scalability
# 
# Design for a larger dataset without creating enterprise-level complexity.
# 
# Ask:
# 
# - What happens with 10 employees?
# - 1,000 employees?
# - 100,000 attendance records?
# - Thousands of leave requests?
# 
# Use:
# 
# - Pagination
# - Filtering
# - Indexing
# - Clear service boundaries
# - Stateless API design where practical
# - Avoidance of unbounded queries
# 
# Do not introduce microservices, queues, Redis, Kubernetes, etc. merely for appearance.
# 
# The hackathon solution should remain understandable.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 24. Reliability
# 
# Every important action should be safe to retry.
# 
# For example:
# 
# ```text
# User clicks Submit
# ↓
# Request takes 2 seconds
# ↓
# User clicks again
# ```
# 
# Avoid duplicate records.
# 
# Disable submit buttons while requests are in flight where appropriate.
# 
# Use database constraints and backend checks for important uniqueness rules.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 25. Attention to Detail
# 
# Before declaring a feature finished, inspect:
# 
# ## UI
# 
# - Spacing
# - Alignment
# - Typography
# - Labels
# - Empty state
# - Loading state
# - Error state
# - Mobile/responsive behavior
# - Long names
# - Large numbers
# - Missing images
# - Long text
# - Date formatting
# - Currency formatting
# 
# ## Logic
# 
# - Boundary dates
# - Duplicate records
# - Invalid states
# - Permissions
# - Refresh behavior
# - Back navigation
# - Browser reload
# - Session expiration
# 
# ## Data
# 
# - Null values
# - Missing relationships
# - Duplicate records
# - Deleted records
# - Stale records
# 
# ---
# 
# # 26. Testing Strategy
# 
# At minimum test:
# 
# ## Authentication
# 
# - Valid login
# - Invalid login
# - Duplicate registration
# - Invalid email
# - Weak password
# - Unverified email
# - Logout
# 
# ## Authorization
# 
# - Employee accessing own data
# - Employee accessing another employee's data
# - Employee calling HR endpoint
# - HR accessing employee data
# 
# ## Attendance
# 
# - Check in
# - Check out
# - Duplicate check in
# - Check out without check in
# - Daily view
# - Weekly view
# 
# ## Leave
# 
# - Valid request
# - Invalid dates
# - Empty request
# - Pending state
# - Approval
# - Rejection
# - Unauthorized approval
# - Already finalized request
# 
# ## Payroll
# 
# - Employee read-only access
# - HR read access
# - HR update
# - Employee attempting update
# 
# ## Integration
# 
# Every major workflow must work:
# 
# ```text
# UI
# → API
# → Database
# → API
# → UI
# ```
# 
# ---
# 
# # 27. Git and Contribution Rules
# 
# All four members must make genuine contributions from their own GitHub accounts.
# 
# Do not create fake commits to inflate numbers.
# 
# Do not split meaningless changes solely to increase commit count.
# 
# Commits should represent logical work units.
# 
# Good:
# 
# ```text
# feat: add leave request workflow
# feat: add attendance API
# feat: add HR employee directory
# fix: prevent duplicate check-in
# ```
# 
# Bad:
# 
# ```text
# change button
# change button again
# fix text
# fix spacing
# test
# test2
# ```
# 
# Before the final code freeze:
# 
# - All meaningful work merged
# - Working `main` branch
# - No broken features from unfinished branches
# - All four members have meaningful history
# 
# ---
# 
# # 28. Commit Quality
# 
# Prefer atomic commits.
# 
# Each commit should:
# 
# - Have a clear purpose
# - Be understandable
# - Avoid unrelated changes
# - Keep the project runnable whenever practical
# 
# Do not commit secrets.
# 
# Do not commit:
# 
# ```text
# .env
# API keys
# passwords
# tokens
# private certificates
# ```
# 
# Commit:
# 
# ```text
# .env.example
# ```
# 
# with placeholders.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 29. Pull Request / Merge Discipline
# 
# Before merging:
# 
# 1. Pull/rebase from current development branch as appropriate.
# 2. Resolve conflicts carefully.
# 3. Verify imports and routes.
# 4. Run relevant checks.
# 5. Verify API compatibility.
# 6. Review database changes.
# 7. Confirm the feature still works end-to-end.
# 
# Avoid large uncontrolled merges near the deadline.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 30. Documentation
# 
# Maintain a useful README.
# 
# It should include:
# 
# - Problem solved
# - Features
# - Architecture
# - Tech stack
# - Database overview
# - Setup
# - Environment variables
# - Run commands
# - API overview
# - Demo credentials if appropriate
# - Known assumptions
# 
# Keep documentation aligned with the actual code.
# 
# Never document features that do not exist.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 31. Hackathon Time Management
# 
# The hackathon manual defines a tactical flow:
# 
# ```text
# 08:30–09:15
# Problem decomposition + ER diagram + API freeze
# 
# 09:15–13:00
# Core MVP CRUD
# 
# 13:00–15:30
# Integration + polish
# 
# 15:30–16:45
# Testing + README + final fixes
# 
# 16:45
# Code freeze
# ```
# 
# Treat the core workflow as non-negotiable.
# 
# Do not spend early hours on animations or advanced analytics while the database and API are incomplete.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 32. Dayflow-Specific Priorities
# 
# For the Dayflow HRMS problem, prioritize:
# 
# ## P0
# 
# - Authentication
# - Role-based access
# - Employee dashboard
# - HR/Admin dashboard
# - Employee profile
# - Attendance
# - Check-in/check-out
# - Leave application
# - Leave approval/rejection
# - Payroll visibility
# - Salary structure update
# - Client + server validation
# - PostgreSQL persistence
# 
# ## P1
# 
# - Employee 360
# - HR Action Center
# - Leave balance/calendar
# - Attendance correction workflow
# - Notifications
# - Audit trail
# - Search/filtering
# - Workforce analytics
# 
# ## P2
# 
# - Advanced email infrastructure
# - Complex payroll calculations
# - Advanced reporting
# - AI chatbot
# - Complex real-time infrastructure
# - Unnecessary external integrations
# 
# Do not build P1/P2 while P0 is unstable.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 33. Dayflow Core Workflow
# 
# The strongest end-to-end demonstration should be:
# 
# ```text
# Employee Login
#       ↓
# Employee Dashboard
#       ↓
# Apply Leave
#       ↓
# Leave = PENDING
#       ↓
# HR Login
#       ↓
# HR sees Leave Request
#       ↓
# Approve / Reject + Comment
#       ↓
# Employee sees updated status
#       ↓
# Database reflects mutation
# ```
# 
# The system must demonstrate the entire chain using real persisted data.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 34. Demo Awareness
# 
# Every feature should be easy to explain.
# 
# Prefer features that demonstrate multiple engineering concepts at once.
# 
# Example:
# 
# ```text
# Leave approval
# ```
# 
# demonstrates:
# 
# - Authentication
# - Authorization
# - UI
# - Form validation
# - API
# - Business logic
# - Status lifecycle
# - Database relationships
# - Persistence
# - Multi-role workflow
# 
# These high-leverage features deserve priority.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 35. Final Engineering Review
# 
# Before code freeze, perform this audit.
# 
# ## Coding Standards
# 
# - [ ] No obvious dead code
# - [ ] No unnecessary libraries
# - [ ] No unused imports
# - [ ] Naming is clear
# - [ ] Functions/components have reasonable size
# - [ ] Responsibilities are separated
# 
# ## Logic
# 
# - [ ] Core workflows work
# - [ ] State transitions are valid
# - [ ] Business rules are enforced server-side
# - [ ] Error cases are handled
# 
# ## Modularity
# 
# - [ ] Reusable components are used
# - [ ] API logic is centralized
# - [ ] Backend business logic is separated
# - [ ] No unnecessary duplication
# 
# ## Frontend
# 
# - [ ] Responsive
# - [ ] Consistent design
# - [ ] Loading states
# - [ ] Empty states
# - [ ] Error states
# - [ ] Success feedback
# - [ ] Accessible forms
# 
# ## Performance
# 
# - [ ] No obvious N+1 queries
# - [ ] Lists are paginated where necessary
# - [ ] No excessive API calls
# - [ ] No unnecessary rerenders
# - [ ] Database indexes are appropriate
# 
# ## Scalability
# 
# - [ ] Large lists will not load everything
# - [ ] Database relationships are sound
# - [ ] No hardcoded data assumptions
# 
# ## Security
# 
# - [ ] Passwords are hashed
# - [ ] Secrets are not committed
# - [ ] Authorization is enforced server-side
# - [ ] User input is validated
# - [ ] Employees cannot access unauthorized records
# - [ ] Sensitive errors are not exposed
# 
# ## Usability
# 
# - [ ] User understands what to do
# - [ ] Forms are clear
# - [ ] Feedback is immediate
# - [ ] Error messages are actionable
# - [ ] Important actions are easy to find
# 
# ## Debugging
# 
# - [ ] Core workflows tested
# - [ ] Edge cases checked
# - [ ] Console errors removed
# - [ ] API errors handled
# - [ ] Refresh/reload tested
# 
# ## Database
# 
# - [ ] Correct relationships
# - [ ] Foreign keys
# - [ ] Unique constraints
# - [ ] Appropriate indexes
# - [ ] No unnecessary duplicated data
# - [ ] Seed data is realistic
# 
# ## Problem Statement
# 
# - [ ] Every required feature is covered
# - [ ] Nothing critical was skipped
# - [ ] Additional features support the actual problem
# - [ ] Future enhancements did not replace core requirements
# 
# ## Attention to Detail
# 
# - [ ] Dates correct
# - [ ] Currency correct
# - [ ] Status labels correct
# - [ ] Long text handled
# - [ ] Empty results handled
# - [ ] Error cases handled
# - [ ] Mobile/responsive layout checked
# 
# ## GitHub
# 
# - [ ] All four members have genuine commits
# - [ ] Commit history reflects real work
# - [ ] No secrets committed
# - [ ] `main` is stable
# - [ ] Final changes pushed before code freeze
# 
# ---
# 
# 
# ---
# 
# # 37. Team Contribution Equity and Private Team Mapping
# 
# The hackathon team has four GitHub identities:
# 
# ```text
# Gamechanger326
# Tejashwii
# bhagmpriyal123-bot
# deveshkolte
# ```
# 
# These identities must be treated as four equal contributors.
# 
# ## Contribution Principle
# 
# The repository should show a **balanced and credible contribution history** across all four members.
# 
# Do not optimize for a high raw commit count by creating meaningless commits.
# 
# Instead, ensure that:
# 
# - All four members make genuine commits.
# - All four members contribute throughout the development period.
# - No single member becomes responsible for nearly all implementation.
# - Commit volume should remain reasonably balanced.
# - Commit size should remain reasonably balanced.
# - Each member should own meaningful functionality.
# - Integration, bug fixes and quality improvements should also be distributed.
# - No member should be used only for documentation or trivial changes.
# - No artificial commits should be created solely to increase the count.
# 
# A reasonable target is to keep the total number of meaningful commits per member within a similar range. The exact number is not a hard requirement; contribution quality and actual ownership are more important.
# 
# ## Private Role Assignment
# 
# The exact mapping between GitHub identity and role should be maintained in a local, non-committed file:
# 
# ```text
# .team-local.md
# ```
# 
# This file is private to the team and must NOT be pushed to GitHub.
# 
# Example local contents:
# 
# ```text
# Gamechanger326       → [assigned role]
# Tejashwii            → [assigned role]
# bhagmpriyal123-bot   → [assigned role]
# deveshkolte          → [assigned role]
# ```
# 
# Do not put private team assignment information into committed source files merely to document who owns which part.
# 
# ## Role Ownership
# 
# Roles should still be divided into substantial ownership areas, for example:
# 
# ```text
# Employee Experience
# HR/Admin Experience
# Backend + Database
# Integration + QA + Demo
# ```
# 
# However, ownership must not become isolation.
# 
# Members should each contribute:
# 
# - Feature implementation
# - Bug fixes
# - Integration
# - Testing
# - Refinement
# - Code review where appropriate
# 
# The goal is a naturally balanced Git history.
# 
# ## Commit Review
# 
# Before code freeze, check:
# 
# ```text
# Member 1 → meaningful commits + feature ownership
# Member 2 → meaningful commits + feature ownership
# Member 3 → meaningful commits + feature ownership
# Member 4 → meaningful commits + feature ownership
# ```
# 
# If one member is significantly behind, assign that member a real remaining engineering task rather than creating artificial commits.
# 
# Never make empty, cosmetic or meaningless commits simply to equalize the numbers.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 38. Private Team Files
# 
# The following local files are team-internal and should not be committed:
# 
# ```text
# .team-local.md
# ```
# 
# The purpose of these files is to keep private contribution mapping, internal assignment notes, or temporary coordination details outside the public repository.
# 
# Do not store credentials, tokens, passwords or secrets in these files either.
# 
# # 39. YAGNI Before Planning
# 
# Before planning or implementing any feature, apply **YAGNI — You Aren't Gonna Need It**.
# 
# Do not design or build functionality merely because:
# 
# - It might be useful later.
# - It is technically interesting.
# - Another product has it.
# - The AI can easily generate it.
# - It would make the architecture look more sophisticated.
# - It may be useful at an unknown future scale.
# 
# Only include a feature when there is a current, defensible reason based on:
# 
# 1. The problem statement.
# 2. A necessary business rule.
# 3. A required user workflow.
# 4. A clear reliability/security/performance requirement.
# 5. A small addition that substantially improves the core product without creating disproportionate complexity.
# 
# For every proposed feature, explicitly answer:
# 
# ```text
# Why is this needed now?
# What requirement does it satisfy?
# What user/business problem does it solve?
# What complexity does it introduce?
# Can the product work correctly without it?
# ```
# 
# If the product works correctly without the feature and the feature is not important to the current scope, do not build it.
# 
# YAGNI applies to:
# 
# - Features
# - APIs
# - Database tables
# - Database fields
# - Dependencies
# - Services
# - Abstractions
# - Infrastructure
# - Design patterns
# - Background jobs
# - Caching
# - Real-time systems
# - Third-party integrations
# 
# Prefer the simplest architecture that satisfies the current requirement.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 40. Explain Before Coding
# 
# **Never begin substantial coding immediately after receiving a feature request.**
# 
# Before writing or modifying code, the AI must first explain the proposed implementation.
# 
# The explanation must be concise enough to be useful during the hackathon but detailed enough for the team to understand what will change.
# 
# For every non-trivial coding task, provide:
# 
# ## 1. Requirement Understanding
# 
# State:
# 
# ```text
# What the user wants
# What problem it solves
# What existing requirement it connects to
# ```
# 
# ## 2. Current-Code Understanding
# 
# Before proposing changes, inspect the relevant existing implementation and explain:
# 
# - Which files/components/modules are involved.
# - How the current flow works.
# - What can be reused.
# - What is currently missing or incorrect.
# 
# Do not invent the existing architecture.
# 
# ## 3. Proposed Logic
# 
# Explain the flow in plain language:
# 
# ```text
# User action
# → validation
# → API
# → business logic
# → database
# → response
# → UI update
# ```
# 
# For workflows, explain all important state transitions.
# 
# ## 4. Files to Change
# 
# List the expected files/modules:
# 
# ```text
# CREATE:
# - ...
# 
# MODIFY:
# - ...
# 
# DELETE:
# - ...
# 
# NO CHANGE:
# - ...
# ```
# 
# Do not modify unrelated files.
# 
# ## 5. Data/API Changes
# 
# If applicable, explain:
# 
# - New/modified database tables
# - New/modified fields
# - Relationships
# - Constraints
# - API endpoints
# - Request/response shapes
# - Authorization requirements
# 
# ## 6. Edge Cases
# 
# Before coding, identify the important failure cases.
# 
# Examples:
# 
# - Empty input
# - Invalid input
# - Duplicate submission
# - Missing record
# - Unauthorized user
# - Invalid status transition
# - API failure
# - Database failure
# - Concurrent update
# 
# ## 7. Security and Performance Impact
# 
# State whether the change introduces or affects:
# 
# - Authorization
# - Sensitive data
# - Input validation
# - Query complexity
# - Index requirements
# - Large payloads
# - Repeated API calls
# - Caching needs
# 
# Do not introduce optimizations without a demonstrated need.
# 
# ## 8. Implementation Plan
# 
# Give a short ordered sequence:
# 
# ```text
# 1. ...
# 2. ...
# 3. ...
# 4. ...
# ```
# 
# Only after this explanation should coding begin.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 41. Mandatory Pre-Coding Gate
# 
# Use this sequence for every significant task:
# 
# ```text
# REQUEST
#    ↓
# READ REQUIREMENT
#    ↓
# YAGNI CHECK
#    ↓
# INSPECT EXISTING CODE
#    ↓
# EXPLAIN CURRENT STATE
#    ↓
# PROPOSE LOGIC
#    ↓
# LIST CHANGES
#    ↓
# IDENTIFY EDGE CASES
#    ↓
# TEAM/USER CONFIRMS PLAN
#    ↓
# IMPLEMENT
#    ↓
# VERIFY
# ```
# 
# The AI must not skip directly from REQUEST → CODE for substantial features.
# 
# For tiny, obvious changes such as a typo fix or one-line configuration correction, the explanation may be brief.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 42. Explain the Code After Coding
# 
# After implementation, do not simply say:
# 
# > "Done."
# 
# Explain what was implemented.
# 
# For each significant change, provide:
# 
# ```text
# What changed
# Why it changed
# How the logic works
# What files were affected
# What was reused
# What was intentionally not changed
# How it was verified
# ```
# 
# Also identify any assumptions made.
# 
# If there are known limitations, state them clearly rather than hiding them.
# 
# ---
# 
# # 43. Code Review Mode
# 
# When reviewing existing code, do not immediately rewrite it.
# 
# First classify findings:
# 
# ```text
# CRITICAL
# HIGH
# MEDIUM
# LOW
# IMPROVEMENT
# ```
# 
# Focus first on:
# 
# - Incorrect business logic
# - Security vulnerabilities
# - Data corruption risk
# - Broken authorization
# - Performance bottlenecks
# - Reliability problems
# - Bugs affecting core workflows
# 
# Do not refactor working code purely for stylistic preference during the hackathon.
# 
# Apply YAGNI to refactoring as well.
# 
# 
# # 36. Final Rule
# 
# When choosing between:
# 
# ```text
# A complex feature that looks impressive
# ```
# 
# and:
# 
# ```text
# A simple feature that works perfectly
# ```
# 
# choose the second one.
# 
# When choosing between:
# 
# ```text
# More features
# ```
# 
# and:
# 
# ```text
# Better correctness, security, usability and polish
# ```
# 
# choose the second one.
# 
# When using AI, never ask:
# 
# > "Can you generate more code?"
# 
# Ask:
# 
# > "What is the simplest, safest and most maintainable implementation that fully satisfies this requirement?"
# 
# Build a product that an evaluator can understand in seconds, trust after five minutes, and inspect technically without finding obvious shortcuts.
